## Step 2 of 5 — `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts` + `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts`

The Zod schema is the single source of truth: it drives runtime validation in `/api/courses/.../lessons` route handlers, the editor form's parse step, and the JSON Schema files emitted by `npm run build:schemas`. Keep it minimal and concrete; use `.refine()` for cross-field invariants.

```ts
// src/widgets/Histogram/schema.ts
import { z } from 'zod';

export const HistogramDataSchema = z
  .object({
    binEdges: z.array(z.number()).min(2),
    counts: z.array(z.number().nonnegative()).min(1),
  })
  .refine((d) => d.binEdges.length === d.counts.length + 1, {
    message: 'binEdges.length must equal counts.length + 1',
    path: ['binEdges'],
  });

export type HistogramData = z.infer<typeof HistogramDataSchema>;
```

Two conventions matter here: **export both the schema and the inferred type** (so the editor and the widget can share types without circular imports), and **declare cross-field invariants with `.refine()`** rather than spreading them across components.

Then register the schema in `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` so `npm run build:schemas` emits `<kebab-case>.json` alongside the other widget JSON Schemas:

```ts
// src/widgets/schemas/build.ts (excerpt)
import { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';

const widgets: Record<string, ZodSchema> = {
  // ... existing entries
  histogram: HistogramDataSchema,
};
```

The key in the `widgets` map becomes the filename: `histogram` → `src/widgets/schemas/histogram.json`. **Do not hand-edit those JSON files** — they are regenerated.

## Step 3 of 5 — `src/widgets/registry.ts`

`registry.ts` is the central type-to-component map. `Widget.tsx` reads it to render the chrome (icon, label, accent rail), and `SectionRenderer` falls through to it for unknown section types.

```ts
// src/widgets/registry.ts (excerpt)
import { BarChart3 } from 'lucide-react';
import { HistogramWidget } from './Histogram/HistogramWidget';

export type WidgetType =
  | 'theory' | 'quiz' | 'code' | 'codeCloze' | 'demo' | 'sandbox'
  | 'histogram'   // ← add to the union
  | 'plotImage' | 'parametricExplorer' | 'dragMatch' | 'dataTable' | 'video'
  | 'custom';

export const widgetRegistry: Record<WidgetType, WidgetRegistryEntry> = {
  // ... existing entries
  histogram: {
    component: HistogramWidget as ComponentType<{ data?: unknown }>,
    label: 'Histogram',
    icon: BarChart3,                    // any lucide-react icon
    accentVar: '--widget-histogram',    // matches Step 1
  },
};
```

Each entry has exactly four fields:

- **`component`** — the read-mode component. The registry erases the per-widget data type to `unknown`; cast inside the component or wrap in a typed adapter.
- **`label`** — the short header eyebrow (e.g. `"HISTOGRAM · §3"`). Keep it ≤ 16 characters.
- **`icon`** — any [`lucide-react`](https://lucide.dev/icons/) icon. Default size 14, no need to pass.
- **`accentVar`** — the CSS variable name **with leading `--`**. Must match Step 1 exactly.

:::callout{type="warning" title="Missing the WidgetType literal"}
Forgetting to add `'histogram'` to the `WidgetType` union is a TypeScript error in the same file: the `Record<WidgetType, ...>` declaration becomes incomplete and the build fails. Always extend the union and the map together.
:::

## Step 4 of 5 — `src/widgets/<Name>/<Name>Editor.tsx`

The editor opens in a 320-px side panel when the user clicks the pencil button on a section header. Each editor is a small controlled form that:

1. Hydrates from `initial: <Type>Data`.
2. Tracks dirty state versus `initial`.
3. Runs `<Type>DataSchema.safeParse` before saving and surfaces issues inline.
4. Calls `onSave(next, sources?)` with the parsed data; the parent persists via `PUT /api/courses/<slug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>`.
5. Calls `onCancel()` to close without saving.

Use the helpers in `@/components/EditorForm` so every editor looks the same:

```tsx
// src/widgets/Histogram/HistogramEditor.tsx (excerpt)
import {
  EditorFormFooter, EditorFormSaveError,
  fieldStyle, formBodyStyle, inputStyle, labelStyle,
  errorTextStyle,
} from '@/components/EditorForm';
import { SourcesField } from '@/components/Sources';
import { HistogramDataSchema, type HistogramData } from './schema';

export function HistogramEditor({ initial, onCancel, onSave }: {
  initial: HistogramData;
  onCancel: () => void;
  onSave: (next: HistogramData) => Promise<void>;
}) {
  // ... state, parse, dirty checks
  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', flex: 1, minHeight: 0 }}>
      <div style={formBodyStyle}>
        <label style={fieldStyle}>
          <span style={labelStyle}>Bin edges (comma-separated)</span>
          <input style={inputStyle} /* ... */ />
        </label>
        {/* ...more fields, then SourcesField... */}
      </div>
      <EditorFormSaveError message={saveError} />
      <EditorFormFooter
        saving={saving}
        saveDisabled={!dirty}
        onCancel={onCancel}
        onSave={() => void handleSave()}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Wire the editor into the lesson page

The editor only mounts after a tiny edit in `src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx`:

- Import the editor at the top of the file.
- Add a branch inside `WidgetEditPanel` that renders the editor when `section?.type === '<name>'` and forwards `onCancel` / `onSave`.
- Add a branch inside `SectionRenderer` that wires the read-mode body and exposes a pencil button so the user can open the panel from the widget header.

Without these two branches, the user can see the widget but cannot edit it from the UI — even though the schema accepts the section.

## Step 5 of 5 — `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts`

Steps 1–4 register the widget for rendering and editing. They do **not** teach the API how to validate a lesson JSON file containing the new widget. Without Step 5, lesson files with the new section type round-trip happily in memory but get rejected by `PUT /api/courses/<slug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>` and by the load-time validator on cold-start.

Add a new section variant to the discriminated union in `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts`:

```ts
// src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts (excerpt)
import { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';

export const HistogramSectionSchema = z.object({
  ...sectionBase,
  type: z.literal('histogram'),
  data: HistogramDataSchema,
});

export const SectionSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('type', [
  // ... existing variants
  HistogramSectionSchema,
]);
```

Three details that catch people out:

- **`...sectionBase`** is a shared shape with `id`, `title`, and optional `sources`. Always spread it first.
- **`type: z.literal('<name>')`** must match the `WidgetType` literal you added in Step 3 character-for-character.
- The variant must be added to the `discriminatedUnion` array, not just declared. Zod uses the array to generate a discriminator-aware parser; a declaration not present in the array is dead code.

:::callout{type="insight" title="Why discriminated union?"}
A discriminated union gives you a per-variant `data` schema typed by the `type` field. That means `HistogramData` validates `data` only when `type === 'histogram'` — no need for a top-level `if/else` on type. The cost is one extra entry in the array per widget; the win is exhaustive type-narrowing in the renderer.
:::
